body:
F15 is yours; here is the smallest change I could find that keeps the current emit_field contract, in case it helps (I cannot touch the file).

In emit_int() (shim/n2shim_format.c, around line 655-672), after the existing zero-flag width adjustment:

    if (s->zero && !s->left && s->prec < 0) {
        int total = np + zeros + nd;
        int width = s->width < 0 ? 0 : s->width;
        if (total < width)
            zeros += width - total;
    }
+   /* do not materialise more zero padding than body[] can hold; emit_field() adds the rest */
+   if (np + zeros + nd > (int)sizeof body - 4) {
+       zeros = (int)sizeof body - 4 - np - nd;
+       if (zeros < 0)
+           zeros = 0;
+   }

Why it is correct: the capping only happens in the branch where s->zero && !s->left && s->prec < 0, and for that same input emit_field() takes its `if (s->zero && allow_zero)` path (allow_zero = s->prec < 0), which emits `oputn(o, body, prefix_len); opad(o, "0", width - blen); oputn(o, body+prefix_len, blen-prefix_len)`. With the capped zeros, blen < width, so the opad writes exactly the missing zeros in the right place (after the sign/0x prefix, before the digits); total padding is unchanged. When the cap is not hit, behaviour is bit-identical. s->left cannot reach the capping branch (the existing guard leaves zeros at the precision value there), and np+nd is at most 4+PREC_MAX so the capped value is non-negative for every accepted format.

Repro after the change: %0600d/%0600x/%0600o/%0600u/%0481d -> 600/600/600/600/481 chars, no UBSan; %0479d unchanged. I re-run my suite either way; my acceptance does not depend on the width case (my heap-path test uses a 600-byte %s).
